<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_pause_circle — pause-button glyph (filled circle + 2 bars)
    ===
    Companion to ic_play_circle.xml. Together they form the
    play/pause toggle pair used in media playback controls:

      - Idle / not playing → ic_play_circle
      - Actively playing   → ic_pause_circle

    Referenced by (Phase 8 — none yet):
      - future: item_audio_preview.xml (inline play/pause
        button in an audio/voice message bubble)
      - future: item_video_preview.xml (overlay toggle on
        the video thumbnail in a video message bubble)
      - future: item_media_viewer_video.xml (overlay toggle
        on the video page in the full-screen media viewer)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF) with cutouts for the two
       pause bars. The actual rendered color comes from the
       consumer's tint:
         - Inline audio-bubble button: ?attr/colorPrimary
           or ?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant.
         - Centered video-overlay button: @android:color/white
           on a semi-transparent black scrim.
       Do NOT set a brand color here — the fill is a template.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT — MATCHES ic_play_circle.xml.
       Same viewport, same outer circle geometry, same
       rendering size. When the play/pause state toggles
       at runtime, the visual transition is a swap of
       internal details (triangle ↔ two bars) with an
       identical outer circle. This is critical for a
       smooth toggle — a play/pause pair with different
       outer circle sizes or positions would produce a
       visible "jump" on toggle that breaks the
       affordance.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL "PAUSE_CIRCLE_FILLED" GLYPH.
       This is Google's canonical Material Design
       "pause_circle_filled" icon. It is the standard
       pause counterpart to play_circle_filled and is
       used by every media player on every platform.
       Do NOT redesign it as a single centered vertical
       bar, as a rotated play triangle, or as an "equals"
       sign — those are all recognizable-but-wrong shapes
       for a pause button.

    4. FILLED CIRCLE, TWO CUT-OUT BARS (evenOdd).
       The circle is filled, and the two vertical bars
       are transparent regions cut out of the circle.
       This mirrors ic_play_circle.xml's construction
       exactly — one evenOdd path with the outer circle
       plus interior cutout contours.

       Do NOT switch to "pause_circle_outline"
       (border-only circle) or "pause" (bars only, no
       circle). Same reasoning as ic_play_circle's note:
       the outline variant disappears on mid-tone
       backgrounds; the bars-only variant is used for
       inline video controls, not as the primary toggle
       affordance.

    5. TWO BARS, EQUAL WIDTH, EQUAL GAP.
       The pause symbol is two vertical rectangles of
       equal width (2 units each), separated by a gap
       equal to their width (2 units). This is the
       universal "pause" proportion — a wider gap reads
       as "stop", a narrower gap reads as "equals sign",
       and unequal bar widths read as broken. Do not
       adjust the widths or gap without also adjusting
       ic_play_circle's triangle to keep the toggle pair
       visually balanced.

    6. BAR HEIGHT IS INTENTIONALLY TALLER THAN THE
       TRIANGLE.
       The bars span y=8 to y=16 (8 units tall), while
       ic_play_circle's triangle spans y=7.5 to y=16.5
       (9 units tall, but only at the triangle's base —
       the triangle's visual mass is smaller because it
       tapers to a point). To compensate for the
       triangle's tapering, the pause bars are drawn
       slightly shorter in each dimension but read as
       visually equivalent to the triangle's apparent
       weight. This is the standard optical-balancing
       adjustment used by Google's Material icons —
       do not "correct" it by making the bars exactly
       match the triangle's bounding box.

    7. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       The pause bars are vertically symmetric about the
       circle's center. Mirroring under RTL produces no
       visible change. Do not add auto-mirroring.

    Rendering result at any size:
      A solid circle with two vertical bars cut out of
      the interior — instant recognition as "tap to
      pause."

    ============================================================
    Runtime toggle pattern (Phase 8 — for reference only)
    ============================================================
      In the audio/video bubble binding code, the
      implementation will look like:

        boolean playing = player.isPlaying();
        button.setImageResource(playing
                ? R.drawable.ic_pause_circle
                : R.drawable.ic_play_circle);

      The two drawables share viewport and outer-circle
      geometry, so the button's size and position are
      preserved across the toggle. The only change is
      the interior glyph. This is why both files use
      identical outer-circle geometry — a mismatch would
      produce a visible scale/position shift on toggle.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Canonical Material Design "pause_circle_filled" path.

        Breakdown:
          Subpath 1 — the outer circle (identical to
          ic_play_circle.xml):
            M 12,2               top of the circle
            C 6.48,2 2,6.48 2,12
                                 cubic from (12,2) to (2,12)
                                 — left half
            s 4.48,10 10,10      mirror cubic to (12,22)
                                 — bottom half
            s 10,-4.48 10,-10    mirror cubic to (22,12)
                                 — right half
            S 17.52,2 12,2       mirror cubic back to
                                 (12,2) — completing the
                                 circle
            z                    close

          Subpath 2 — left pause bar (cutout):
            M 11,16              start at the bar's
                                 bottom-right corner
            H 9                  left 2 units (bar's
                                 bottom-left corner)
            V 8                  up 8 units (bar's
                                 top-left corner)
            h 2                  right 2 units (bar's
                                 top-right corner)
            V 16                 down 8 units (closing
                                 the bar at its
                                 bottom-right)
            z                    close

            Rectangle from (9,8) to (11,16) — 2 units
            wide, 8 units tall, vertically centered
            (y=12 is the midpoint).

          Subpath 3 — right pause bar (cutout):
            M 15,16              start at the bar's
                                 bottom-right corner
            h -2                 left 2 units (bar's
                                 bottom-left corner)
            V 8                  up 8 units (bar's
                                 top-left corner)
            h 2                  right 2 units (bar's
                                 top-right corner)
            V 16                 down 8 units (closing
                                 the bar)
            z                    close

            Rectangle from (13,8) to (15,16) — 2 units
            wide, 8 units tall. The gap between the two
            bars is x=11 to x=13 (2 units), equal to each
            bar's width.

        Under evenOdd fill, the circle is filled (the
        tint color) and the two bars are cut out
        (transparent). The result is the standard
        "pause button" glyph.

        Do NOT change fillType to nonZero. The bars would
        fill in the same color as the circle, producing
        a solid circle with no visible pause symbol.
        This is the same reasoning as ic_play_circle's
        evenOdd note — the cutout depends on it.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:fillType="evenOdd"
        android:pathData="M12,2C6.48,2 2,6.48 2,12s4.48,10 10,10 10,-4.48 10,-10S17.52,2 12,2zM11,16H9V8h2V16zM15,16h-2V8h2V16z" />

</vector>
